email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\n1. **`lower(u.email)` blocks the index (most important).** Wrapping the column in a function means a normal index on `users.email` can't be used. The database scans the whole `users` table and lowercases every row.\n   - **Fix, option A:** create an expression index with `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));`. This works on Postgres, Oracle and MySQL 8+ (where it needs the double-parenthesis form, `((lower(email)))`).\n   - **Fix, option B:** store emails already lowercased, or use a case-insensitive type or collation (`citext` on Postgres, or MySQL's default `_ci` collations). Then use `WHERE u.email = ...` with a plain index.\n   - **Fix, option C:** on SQL Server, the default collation is already case-insensitive, so drop `lower()` and index `email`.\n\n2. **`SELECT *` returns more than you need.** It pulls every column from both tables, including any wide text or JSON columns. It also duplicates column names like `id` and `created_at`. It blocks index-only scans, and it breaks callers if the schema changes. List only the columns you need.\n\n3. **`orders.user_id` may have no index.** Once the user is found, the join looks up that user's orders by `o.user_id`. Without an index on it, that's a full scan of `orders`. Make sure `CREATE INDEX ON orders (user_id)` exists. A foreign key doesn't create one automatically on Postgres or Oracle.\n\n4. **No `LIMIT` or pagination.** If a user can have many orders, the result is unbounded. Add `ORDER BY` with `LIMIT` if the caller doesn't need every order.\n\n5. **Uniqueness (a smaller point).** If `users.email` has a unique constraint or index on the lowercased value, the planner knows the match is at most one user and can use a cheap nested-loop join. Without it, the planner may overestimate row counts and pick a worse plan.\n\nSuggested rewrite, assuming a `lower(email)` expression index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.name\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org'\nORDER BY o.created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n```\n\nI only had this one query. I couldn't check the schema, existing indexes, table sizes or an `EXPLAIN` plan. Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE)` before and after adding the indexes to confirm the gain.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}